Put spaces around symbolic operators.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    total = left + right
    count += 1
]

Moth uses words for equality and logic:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    same = left is right
    different = left is not right
    ready = loaded and valid
    retry = timed_out or disconnected
    blocked = not ready
]

Division has two forms:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    real_result = 5 / 2
    whole_result = 5 // 2
]

`/` produces a real `Float` result. `//` performs integer division and produces
an `Int`.

Use templates to join text:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    greeting = ["Hello, ", "Priya"]
]

Templates handle all string joining and interpolation. `+` is for numbers
only.

### Typed widths

`Int`, `Uint` and `Float` are the ordinary arithmetic types. `Uint` follows the
selected `Int` width and checks its unsigned range. `Uint` and `Int` need an
explicit conversion to mix, even for arithmetic, while `Uint` and `Float`
mix to `Float` and `Uint` values compare exactly with `Int` values.
Fixed-width values do not
mix with them or with each other silently. A fixed-width operator first promotes
both operands to a common type chosen from their complete ranges, and some pairs,
such as `I64` and `U64`, have no common type and are rejected. Fixed integer `/`
produces `F64`, while `//` stays integer division.

Unary minus is invalid on every `U*` and `Uint` value, including a known zero. Convert to a
signed type first. `Byte` takes part in no arithmetic at all.

A `Dec` or `Uint` receiver types unresolved literal arithmetic before operator selection, with different exponent rules. See @./#numeric-literals (Numeric literals) for that contract.

A compound assignment such as `+=` computes in the promoted type and then checks
that the result fits the left-hand type. It writes only after both checks succeed.
A `catch` on the right-hand expression does not catch these later update checks.
See @./#checked-arithmetic (Checked arithmetic) for failure delivery.

Fixed-width promotion, compatibility checks and numeric constant folding are
implemented in the frontend/HIR. HTML-JS executes fixed-width, `Byte`, `Uint` and
`Dec` runtime values. HTML-Wasm executes supported fixed integers, `Uint`
scalars through `U32`/`U64` carriers in trap-mode-supported paths, `Byte`
and fixed binary float values; any operation that can actually fail and needs
recoverable delivery is rejected with a capability diagnostic, while
operations proven unable to fail, such as `U8 + U8 -> U32`, compile. `F16`
arithmetic uses a finite `F32` computation carrier. Wasm rejects reachable
`Dec` values. See @../progress (Progress) for current support.
